\f1\fs22 Hayley was avoiding showing up at Sarah\'92s apartment: she wanted nothing less than to tip off Shakti that she was shadowing his investigation, as shambolic as she was sure it would be.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It didn\'92t help that Sarah\'92s apartment building was reachable only by hover-copt. There weren\'92t any liveable floors in or below the Layer, only a cluster of load-bearing pylons reaching for the ground. The design and placement of the pylons seemed random, until you understood that the property developer required that the building\'92s support structure inflicted maximum damage on the Cloudborne community living underneath. Many of the buildings down there appeared as if grafted onto the pylons, whereas the truth was, the pylons pierced through pre-existing crumbling tenements and broken schools. In one case, a pylon completely obliterated all but seventeen seats in the main grandstand of the local rugby club and, seemingly without purpose, jutted out onto the pitch, encroaching on the in-goal area at one end. A sprinting winger scoring a try in that corner risked dashing their brains out on the concrete bluff five metres behind the goal line.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The pylon design won that year\'92s award for Most Brutal Brutalist Structure Occasioning Cloudborne Death. The principal engineer was awarded a knighthood in the Order of the Brutish Empire.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A hover-copt trip to Jacobson\'92s apartment building might trigger an alert; then again, Shakti may be too incompetent to care. Hayley elected to risk it. Who knows? Maybe Jacobson was in-residence, her disappearance a politically motivated beat-up.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley told the concierge in the luxurious foyer she was there to see Sarah Jacobson, and was told she was not at home.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, I feel so silly,\'92 Hayley told the concierge, who was more attentive to some Xtreme Fighting Porn simulation she was playing on her tablet. \'91I simply could not believe she\'92d been kidnapped,\'92 Hayley went on.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The concierge\'97
\fs18 T\'92ING O\'92MUGDEN
\fs22 , by her name tag\'97looked up from her tablet. \'91Kidnapped?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I know, right? We\'92d arranged some shopping therapy for today, and I\'85 well, I guess I was in denial.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The concierge sceptically studied Hayley\'92s outfit. \'91You and Ms Jacobson shop together?\'92 She lifted her chin and afforded Hayley a view up her nostrils.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, yes, but I\'92m surprised you seem to know nothing about this kidnapping story. You must have seen police crawling all over the place.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92ve seen no police,\'92 the concierge said, her demeanour ossifying.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley took a breath. If Shakti hadn\'92t bothered to check out Jacobson\'92s apartment, perhaps he\'92d never notice if Hayley had. \'91Well, you\'92ve seen police now,\'92 she said and flashed her badge from her tablet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah Jacobson was not home. The apartment smelled of cleaning chemicals, and every piece of furniture, every throw pillow, every curio, every picture on the wall possessed the authority of precise placement and the presentability of a fresh hotel suite. There were no cups or dishes drying on the sink, no clothing in the laundry hamper. The shower was bone dry.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Everything was perfectly in place. Except for one item. On the end of the kitchen bench\'97as if it was there as a reminder to pick it up on the way out\'97was a budget Floormart-brand six-pack of boxer shorts. They were pink and had cute cartoon fairies on them.\

